'''Edward Miner Lamont Jr.'''<ref>{{cite news| url= https://www.courant.com/politics/capitol-watch/hc-pol-edward-ned-lamont-20181212-story.html| title= Ned or Edward? Lamont keeps it informal as governor| first= Daniela| last= Altimari| date= December 12, 2018| access-date= June 10, 2019| work= Hartford Courant}}</ref> ({{IPAc-en|l|ə|ˈ|m|ɒ|n|t}} {{respell|lə|MONT}};<ref>{{cite AV media |date= November 9, 2021|title= Gov. Ned Lamont announces reelection campaign for 2022 race| url= https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=U1fDrDnQhhg| publisher= Fox 61| via= [[YouTube]]| access-date= August 20, 2024 |time= 00:04}}</ref> born January 3, 1954) is an American politician and businessman serving since 2019 as the 89th [[governor of Connecticut]].<ref>{{cite news| url= https://greenwich.dailyvoice.com/neighbors/happy-birthday-to-greenwichs-ned-lamont-jr/552594/| title= Happy Birthday to Greenwich's Ned Lamont Jr.| work= Greenwich Daily Voice| date= January 3, 2014| access-date= August 15, 2018}}</ref><ref name= "Courant2018Win">{{cite news| last1= Vigdor| first1= Neil| last2= Kovner| first2= Josh| last3= Lender| first3= Jon| last4= Ormseth| first4= Matthew| last5= Megan| first5= Kathleen| last6= Rondinone| first6= Nicholas| title= Bob Stefanowski Concedes Governor's Race After Cities Push Ned Lamont To Victory| url= https://www.courant.com/politics/elections/hc-election-governor-connecticut-lamont-stefanowski-20181101-story.html| work= [[Hartford Courant]]| date= November 7, 2018| access-date= November 7, 2018}}</ref> A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], he served from 1987 to 1989 as a [[Greenwich, Connecticut|Greenwich]] [[Board of selectmen|selectman]], and was the Democratic nominee for the [[United States Senate]] in [[2006 United States Senate election in Connecticut|2006]], losing to [[Connecticut for Lieberman|independent]] incumbent [[Joe Lieberman]].<ref name= Lamont2018/>{{efn|Lamont defeated Lieberman in the Democratic primary, but Lieberman won the general election running as an [[Connecticut for Lieberman|independent]] candidate.}}

Lamont ran for governor in [[2010 Connecticut gubernatorial election|2010]] but lost the Democratic primary to former [[Stamford, Connecticut|Stamford]] [[Mayor of Stamford, Connecticut|mayor]] [[Dannel Malloy]], who won the general election. He ran again in [[2018 Connecticut gubernatorial election|2018]], winning the nomination and defeating Republican [[Bob Stefanowski]] in the general election.<ref name=Lamont2018>{{cite web|last=Blair|first=Russell|url=http://www.courant.com/politics/hc-pol-ned-lamont-governors-race-20180117-story.html|title=Ned Lamont Jumps Into Connecticut Governor's Race|work=[[Hartford Courant]]|date=January 17, 2018|access-date=January 17, 2018}}</ref> He faced Stefanowski again in [[2022 Connecticut gubernatorial election|2022]], defeating him by a wider margin.<ref name="second term"/> As governor, Lamont signed legislation [[Legalization of non-medical cannabis in the United States|legalizing cannabis]],<ref>{{Cite news|last=Wong|first=Ashley|date=June 22, 2021|title=Connecticut Legalizes Recreational Marijuana, With Sales Aimed for 2022|language=en-US|work=The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2021/06/22/nyregion/connecticut-weed-legalization.html|access-date=November 6, 2021|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> [[sports betting]], and [[online gambling]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Singer|first=Stephen|title=Gov. Ned Lamont and tribal owners of the Mohegan Sun and Foxwoods strike a deal bringing sports betting and online gambling to Connecticut|url=https://www.courant.com/politics/hc-pol-online-gambling-tribes-agreement-20210318-kyxv7aogsnabxlqre746wkkzg4-story.html|access-date=November 6, 2021|website=courant.com|date=March 18, 2021 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=June 26, 2019|title=Lamont signs new budget, says it will bring stability to CT|url=https://ctmirror.org/2019/06/26/lamont-signs-new-budget-says-it-will-bring-stability-to-ct/|access-date=November 6, 2021|website=The CT Mirror|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|date=June 9, 2021|title=CT House passes $46B budget that avoids tax hikes|url=https://ctmirror.org/2021/06/08/house-expected-to-pass-46b-budget-that-avoids-tax-hikes/|access-date=November 6, 2021|website=The CT Mirror|language=en-US}}</ref> In 2025, Lamont vetoed legislation that would affect housing supply in Connecticut.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news |date=2025 |title=Emails show Lamont considered signing Connecticut housing bill days before vetoing it |url=https://www.ctinsider.com/politics/article/ned-lamont-ct-housing-bill-veto-emails-21131452.php| url-access = subscription |work=Connecticut Insider}}</ref>

==Early life and education==
Lamont was born on January 3, 1954, in [[Washington, D.C.]], to Camille Helene (née Buzby) and Edward Miner Lamont. His mother was born in [[San Juan, Puerto Rico]], to parents from the U.S. mainland, and later worked as a staffer for Senator [[Estes Kefauver]].<ref name="Legacy">{{cite news|url=http://www.legacy.com/obituaries/nytimes/obituary.aspx?pid=173841282 |title=Camille Lamont Obituary|newspaper=The New York Times|date=January 14, 2015|access-date=October 9, 2016}}</ref> His father, an economist, worked on the [[Marshall Plan]] and served in the [[United States Department of Housing and Urban Development|Department of Housing and Urban Development]] during the [[Presidency of Richard Nixon|Nixon administration]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.thenation.com/article/fight-partys-soul|title=A Fight for the Party's Soul|first=John|last=Nichols|work=[[The Nation]]|date=July 27, 2006|access-date=February 8, 2013}}</ref> He is the great-grandson of former [[J. P. Morgan & Co.]] chair [[Thomas W. Lamont]]<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.wargs.com/other/lamont.html|title=Ancestry of Ned Lamont|first=William Addams|last=Reitwiesner|access-date=February 8, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.the40yearplan.com/article_012406.php|work=The 40-Year Plan|title=Ned Lamont (interview)|first=Ken|last=Krayeske|date=January 24, 2006|access-date=August 5, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070225081606/http://www.the40yearplan.com/article_012406.php|archive-date=February 25, 2007}}</ref> and a grand-nephew of former [[American Civil Liberties Union]] director and [[National Council of American–Soviet Friendship]] chairman and founder [[Corliss Lamont]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.corliss-lamont.org/|title=The Life of Corliss Lamont|date=May 27, 2018|access-date=August 15, 2018|publisher=Half-Moon Foundation, Inc.}}</ref> He is a distant descendant of colonial diarist [[Thomas Minor]], from whom he gets his middle name.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2006/10/15/opinion/nyregionopinions/15CT_Sleeper.html|title=The American Lamonts|work=The New York Times|first=Jim|last=Sleeper|access-date=February 27, 2019|date=October 15, 2006}}</ref>{{efn|The spelling of Minor's surname has historically varied between ''Minor'' and ''Miner''.<ref name=Curious>Miner, John A. and Miner, Robert F. "The Curious Pedigree of Lt. Thomas Minor". ''New England Historical and Genealogical Register''. New England Historic Genealogical Society. July 1984, pg 182-185. Accessed July 14, 2007.</ref>
}}

Lamont's family moved to [[Laurel Hollow, New York|Laurel Hollow]] on [[Long Island]] when he was seven years old.{{efn|Some media outlets erroneously report Lamont's childhood home as [[Syosset, New York]] due to its shared [[ZIP Code]] (11791) with Laurel Hollow.<ref name="Kershaw"/><ref name="Legacy"/>}} The eldest of three children, he and his sisters attended [[East Woods School]].<ref name="Legacy"/><ref name="Kershaw">{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2006/11/01/nyregion/01lamont.html|title=A Son of Privilege Takes His Baby Steps on the Political Proving Ground|work=The New York Times|first1=Sarah|last1=Kershaw|first2=Alison Leigh|last2=Cowan|date=November 1, 2006|access-date=November 9, 2018}}</ref> He later attended [[Phillips Exeter Academy]], and served as president of the student newspaper, ''[[The Exonian]]''. After graduating from Phillips Exeter in 1972, he earned a Bachelor of Arts in sociology from [[Harvard College]] in 1976 and a [[Master of Business Administration]] from the [[Yale School of Management]] in 1980.<ref name="Kershaw"/><ref name="CT mirror">{{cite web|url=http://www.ctmirror.org/node/6058/candidate|title=Ned Lamont: Democrat candidate for Governor|work=[[The Connecticut Mirror]]|access-date=August 5, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100704084345/http://www.ctmirror.org/node/6058/candidate|archive-date=July 4, 2010}}</ref>

==Professional career==
In 1977, Lamont became editor for the ''[[Black River Tribune]]'', a small weekly newspaper in [[Ludlow (village), Vermont|Ludlow, Vermont]]. During his time there, he worked alongside journalists [[Jane Mayer]] and [[Alex Beam]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.courant.com/2010/08/02/unknown-no-longer-lamont-runs-for-governor/|title=Unknown No Longer, Lamont Runs For Governor|first=Christopher|last=Keating|newspaper=Hartford Courant|date=August 2, 2010|access-date=August 28, 2018|archive-date=August 29, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180829001107/http://articles.courant.com/2010-08-02/news/hc-primary-gov-ned-lamont-0803-20100802_1_stamford-mayor-dannel-malloy-bitter-race-republicans-and-democrats|url-status=live}}</ref> After graduating from Yale, he entered the cable television industry, managing the startup operation in [[Fairfield County, Connecticut]], for [[Cablevision]].<ref name="CT mirror"/> In 1984, he founded Campus Televideo, a company that provides cable and satellite services to college campuses across the country.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.hartfordbusiness.com/article/20180720/NEWHAVENBIZ/180729989/lamont-in-the-lions-den|title=Lamont in the lion's den|work=New Haven Biz|date=July 20, 2018|first=Michael C.|last=Bingham|access-date=August 29, 2018|archive-date=August 29, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180829175603/http://www.hartfordbusiness.com/article/20180720/NEWHAVENBIZ/180729989/lamont-in-the-lions-den|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name=Keating>{{cite news|url=http://www.courant.com/politics/capitol-watch/hc-pol-lamont-sold-cable-tv-company-20180119-story.html|title=After Selling Cable Company, Ned Lamont is 'All In'|first=Christopher|last=Keating|newspaper=Hartford Courant|date=January 19, 2018|access-date=August 29, 2018}}</ref> He later chaired Lamont Digital Systems, a telecommunications firm that invests in [[new media]] startups.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/profiles/companies/9889540Z:US-lamont-digital-systems-inc|title=Lamont Digital Systems Inc|publisher=Bloomberg L.P.|access-date=August 28, 2018}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.ctpost.com/politics/article/Ned-Lamont-s-cable-business-had-an-MTV-13111577.php|title=Ned Lamont's cable business launched with tip from MTV|first=Dan|last=Haar|newspaper=Connecticut Post|date=July 28, 2018|access-date=August 28, 2018|archive-date=May 15, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210515224058/https://www.ctpost.com/politics/article/Ned-Lamont-s-cable-business-had-an-MTV-13111577.php|url-status=dead}}</ref> Campus Televideo was its largest division before [[Austin, Texas]]-based firm Apogee acquired it on September 3, 2015.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.marketwired.com/press-release/apogee-acquires-campus-televideo-becomes-higher-educations-largest-resnet-video-solutions-2052678.htm|title=Apogee Acquires Campus Televideo -- Becomes Higher Education's Largest ResNet and Video Solutions Provider|date=September 3, 2015|work=Market Wired}}</ref><ref name=Keating/>

Lamont has volunteered at [[Warren Harding High School]] in [[Bridgeport, Connecticut|Bridgeport]], teaching entrepreneurship and coordinating internships with local businesses.<ref>{{cite news |last=Lockhart |first=Brian |date=June 13, 2018 |title=Fact checking Lamont's ties to Harding High |url=https://www.ctpost.com/news/article/Testing-Lamont-s-ties-to-Harding-High-12991863.php |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200929173614/https://www.ctpost.com/news/article/Testing-Lamont-s-ties-to-Harding-High-12991863.php |archive-date=September 29, 2020 |access-date=October 29, 2018 |work=Connecticut Post}}</ref><ref name="CONEG">{{cite web |title=Edward Miner "Ned" Lamont, Jr., Connecticut Governor |url=https://www.coneg.org/governor/ned-lamont/ |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190818195539/https://www.coneg.org/governor/ned-lamont/ |archive-date=August 18, 2019 |access-date=November 22, 2019 |work=Coalition of Northeastern Governors}}</ref> He has been a teaching fellow at the [[Harvard Institute of Politics]]<ref>{{cite web |title=Former Fellows-The Institute of Politics |url=http://www.iop.harvard.edu/former-fellows/L |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121022154552/http://www.iop.harvard.edu/former-fellows/L |archive-date=October 22, 2012 |access-date=February 8, 2013 |work=[[Harvard Institute of Politics]] |publisher=[[Harvard University]]}}</ref> and the Yale School of Management, and an adjunct faculty member and chair of the Arts and Sciences Public Policy Committee at [[Central Connecticut State University]] (CCSU), where he was named Distinguished Professor of Political Science and Philosophy.<ref name="CONEG" /> During his time at CCSU, he was a lecturer in multiple classes and founded a business startup competition.<ref name="CONEG" /><ref>{{cite news |last=Krasselt |first=Kaitlyn |date=October 21, 2018 |title=Ned Lamont's eight-year break from politics |url=https://www.ctpost.com/politics/article/Ned-Lamont-s-eight-year-break-from-politics-13321174.php |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211023232902/https://www.ctpost.com/politics/article/Ned-Lamont-s-eight-year-break-from-politics-13321174.php |archive-date=October 23, 2021 |access-date=November 22, 2019 |work=CT Post}}</ref> In 2019, he delivered CCSU's [[commencement speech]], his first as governor.<ref>{{cite news |date=May 12, 2019 |title=Gov. Ned Lamont to deliver commencement remarks at CCSU |url=https://www.apnews.com/a358fc64e1924613bd18ac8c9029d11e |access-date=June 7, 2019 |work=Associated Press}}</ref>

Lamont has served on the [[board of trustees]] for the [[Conservation Services Group]],<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nedlamont.com/about|title=About Ned|access-date=November 29, 2018|publisher=Ned for CT|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181021151512/https://www.nedlamont.com/about|archive-date=October 21, 2018|url-status=dead}}</ref> [[Mercy Corps]],<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.mercycorps.org/articles/iraq/ned-lamont-calls-out-mercy-corps-work-huffington-post|title=Ned Lamont calls out Mercy Corps' work on The Huffington Post|work=Mercy Corps|first=Joy|last=Portella|date=June 30, 2009|access-date=August 29, 2018}}</ref> the [[Norman Rockwell Museum]],<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nrm.org/2011/09/norman-rockwell-museum-announces-new-board-members/|title=Norman Rockwell Museum Announces New Board Members|work=[[Norman Rockwell Museum]]|date=September 9, 2011|access-date=February 8, 2013}}</ref> the [[YMCA]], and the [[Young Presidents' Organization]].<ref name="moffly">{{cite web|url=https://ilovefc.com/2006/04/19/ned-lamont-makes-a-run-for-the-us-senate/|title=Ned Lamont Makes a Run for the US Senate|date=April 19, 2006|publisher=Moffly Media|website=ilovefc.com|access-date=August 15, 2018}}</ref> He has also served on the advisory boards of the [[Brookings Institution]] and the [[Yale School of Management]].<ref name="moffly"/><ref>{{cite web|url=https://som.yale.edu/community/school-leadership-boards/boards-councils/yale-som-board-of-advisors|title=Yale SOM Board of Advisors|work=Yale School of Management|date=May 26, 2013|publisher=Yale University|access-date=August 15, 2018}}</ref>

==Early political career==
{{see also|Electoral history of Ned Lamont}}
Lamont was first elected in 1987 as a [[selectman]] in [[Greenwich, Connecticut]], where he served for one term. He ran for [[Connecticut Senate|state senate]] in 1990, against Republican [[William H. Nickerson|William Nickerson]] and incumbent [[Emil Benvenuto]] (who had switched his party affiliation from Republican to [[A Connecticut Party (1990)|A Connecticut Party]]).<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.courant.com/politics/capitol-watch/hc-greenwich-mourns-death-of-former-sen-bennie-benvenuto-ran-against-ned-lamont-and-bill-nickerson-in-1990-senate-race-20111012-story.html|title=Greenwich Mourns Death Of Former Sen. Bennie Benvenuto; Ran Against Ned Lamont And Bill Nickerson In Three-Way 1990 State Senate Race|work=Hartford Courant|first=Christopher|last=Keating|date=October 12, 2011|access-date=January 2, 2019|archive-date=April 12, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190412155532/https://www.courant.com/politics/capitol-watch/hc-greenwich-mourns-death-of-former-sen-bennie-benvenuto-ran-against-ned-lamont-and-bill-nickerson-in-1990-senate-race-20111012-story.html|url-status=dead}}</ref> Nickerson won the three-way race with Lamont finishing third.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.ourcampaigns.com/RaceDetail.html?RaceID=697193|publisher=Our Campaigns|title=CT State Senate 36|access-date=March 4, 2020}}</ref> Lamont later served for three terms on the Greenwich town finance board and chaired the State Investment Advisory Council, which oversees [[state pension]] fund investments.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.myleftnutmeg.com/showDiary.do?diaryId=237|work=MyLeftNutmeg|title=Lamont Grants MyLeftNutmeg First Blogger Interview|date=January 13, 2006|access-date=August 3, 2006|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20060613193545/http://www.myleftnutmeg.com/showDiary.do?diaryId=237|archive-date=June 13, 2006}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://portal.ct.gov/governor/about-governor-ned-lamont?language=en_US|title=About Governor Ned Lamont|work=The Office of Governor Ned Lamont|access-date=September 3, 2020}}</ref>

===2006 U.S. Senate election===
{{main|2006 United States Senate election in Connecticut}}

[[File:2006 United States Senate election in Connecticut results map by municipality.svg|thumb|General election results by municipality. Shades of blue denote wins for Lamont, yellow for Lieberman.]]
[[File:Ned Lamont in 2006 (cropped).jpg|thumb|left|150px|Lamont in 2006]]
[[File:Ned Lamont YearlyKos 2007 (1000450845).jpg|thumb|220px|Lamont attending the [[Netroots Nation#2007|2007 YearlyKos]] conference at the [[McCormick Place]] in Chicago]]
On March 13, 2006, Lamont announced his campaign for the U.S. Senate against incumbent [[Joe Lieberman]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://yaledailynews.com/blog/2006/04/06/lamont-courts-local-voters/|title=Lamont courts local voters|first=Melina|last=Cordero|date=April 6, 2006|access-date=February 8, 2013|work=[[Yale Daily News]]}}</ref> On July 6, Lamont debated Lieberman on television, covering issues such as the [[Iraq War]], energy policy, and immigration. During the debate, Lieberman argued he was being subjected to a [[Litmus test (politics)|litmus test]] on the war, insisted he was a "bread-and-butter Democrat", and repeatedly asked, "Who is Ned Lamont?" Lieberman also asked Lamont if he would release his [[income tax]] returns, which he did afterward.<ref name="NBCFaceOff">{{cite web|url=http://www.nbc30.com/politics/9481028/detail.html|title=Lieberman, Lamont Face Off In NBC 30 Debate|work=[[WVIT]]|date=July 6, 2006|access-date=August 5, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071225190159/http://www.nbc30.com/politics/9481028/detail.html|archive-date=December 25, 2007}}</ref>

Lamont focused on Lieberman's supportive relationship with Republicans, telling him "if you won't challenge [[George W. Bush|President Bush]] and his failed agenda, I will." He criticized Lieberman's vote for the [[Energy Policy Act of 2005]], which he dubbed the "Bush–[[Dick Cheney|Cheney]]–Lieberman energy bill." In response to the assertion that he supported Republican policies, Lieberman stated he had voted with [[Senate Democratic Caucus|Senate Democrats]] 90% of the time. Lamont argued the three-term incumbent lacked the courage to challenge the [[Presidency of George W. Bush|Bush administration]] on the Iraq War.<ref name="NBCFaceOff"/> He also criticized Lieberman for supporting federal intervention in the [[Terri Schiavo case]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://inthesetimes.com/article/2549/why_ned_lamont_is_a_democrat|title=Why Ned Lamont is a Democrat|date=March 21, 2006|last=Seder|first=Sam|access-date=August 15, 2018|work=In These Times}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.truthdig.com/articles/ned-lamont-the-truthdig-interview/|title=Ned Lamont: The Truthdig Interview|last=Golson|first=Blair|date=April 26, 2006|access-date=August 15, 2018|work=TruthDig}}</ref>

On July 30, ''[[The New York Times]]'' editorial board endorsed Lamont.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2006/07/30/opinion/30sun1.html?_r=1&oref=slogin|title=A Senate Race in Connecticut|date=July 30, 2006|access-date=August 15, 2018|newspaper=The New York Times}}</ref> The same day, ''[[The Sunday Times (UK)|The Sunday Times]]'' reported that [[Bill Clinton]] warned Lieberman not to run as an independent if he lost the primary to Lamont.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.thetimes.com/world/us-world/article/the-anti-war-tycoon-splits-democrats-k0fq0w57gfl|work=[[The Sunday Times (UK)|The Sunday Times]]|title=The anti-war tycoon splits Democrats|first=Tony|last=Allen-Mills|date=July 30, 2006|access-date=August 3, 2006}}</ref> Pledging to refuse money from [[lobbyist]]s during the election, Lamont funded most of his own campaign, with donations exceeding $12.7 million.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2006/10/21/AR2006102101016.html|title=Lamont Gives $2M to Flagging Campaign|first=Andrew|last=Miga|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|date=October 21, 2006|access-date=February 8, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://lamontblog.blogspot.com/2006/04/4000-donors-in-first-quarter.html|title=4,000 Donors in First Quarter|date=April 3, 2006|access-date=August 5, 2013|work=LamontBlog|first=Ned|last=Lamont}}</ref>

Lamont won the primary with 52% of the vote.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://hosted.ap.org/dynamic/files/elections/2006/by_state/CT_Page_0808.html?SITE=CTHARELN&SECTION=POLITICS|title=Connecticut primary results|newspaper=Hartford Courant|date=August 10, 2006|access-date=August 5, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130325075023/http://hosted.ap.org/dynamic/files/elections/2006/by_state/CT_Page_0808.html?SITE=CTHARELN&SECTION=POLITICS|archive-date=March 25, 2013}}</ref> (This was the only [[2006 United States Senate elections|Senate race in 2006]] where an incumbent lost re-nomination.) In his concession speech, Lieberman announced he was standing by his earlier statements that he would run as an independent if he lost the primary.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.nbcnews.com/id/wbna14228351|title=Lieberman concedes; Lamont wins primary|date=August 9, 2006|access-date=February 8, 2013|work=[[NBC News]]}}</ref> Running under the banner of [[Connecticut for Lieberman]], Lieberman won the general election with nearly 50% of the vote (exit polls showed Lieberman won 33% of Democrats, 54% of independents, and 70% of Republicans).<ref>{{cite news| url=http://www.cnn.com/ELECTION/2006/pages/results/states/CT/S/01/epolls.0.html|work=[[CNN]]|title=America Votes 2006: Exit Polls|access-date=August 5, 2013}}</ref> The [[Sundance TV|Sundance Channel]] documentary film ''Blog Wars'' chronicled the influence [[political blogging]] had on the election.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.powerlineblog.com/archives/2006/12/016037.php|first=John|last=Hinderaker|date=December 26, 2006|access-date=March 4, 2019|title=Blog Wars|work=Power Line}}</ref>

While some [[Research 2000]] polls commissioned by the [[Daily Kos]] in 2007 and 2008 found he would win a Senate rematch with Lieberman by growing margins,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.dailykos.com/story/2008/04/07/491459/-CT-Sen-Lieberman-s-popularity-continues-to-slide|work=[[Daily Kos]]|title=CT-Sen: Lieberman's popularity continues to slide|date=April 7, 2008|access-date=August 5, 2013|first=Markos|last=Moulitsas}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://thehill.com/blogs/blog-briefing-room/news/campaigns/40236-poll-lieberman-would-lose-to-lamont/|title=Poll: Lieberman Would Lose to Lamont|work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]]|first=Walter|last=Alarkon|date=July 6, 2008|access-date=February 8, 2013}}</ref> Lamont said he was not considering another campaign for Senate.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.ctmirror.org/story/2010/12/07/lamont-not-looking-rematch-lieberman-2012|title=Lamont not looking for a rematch with Lieberman in 2012|work=Connecticut Mirror|first=Mark|last=Pazniokas|date=December 8, 2010|access-date=August 5, 2013}}</ref> Research 2000 was later accused by the Daily Kos of fabricating their polling data. A lawsuit ended in 2012 with a default judgement against Research 2000.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Kos Media LLC et al v. Research 2000 et al |url=https://dockets.justia.com/docket/california/candce/3:2010cv02894/229290 |access-date=March 12, 2022 |website=Justia Dockets & Filings |language=en}}</ref>

===2008 presidential campaign activity===
Lamont initially supported [[Chris Dodd]]'s [[Chris Dodd 2008 presidential campaign|presidential campaign]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.huffingtonpost.com/ari-melber/ned-lamont-backs-habeas-c_b_42069.html|title=Ned Lamont Backs Habeas Corpus- and Chris Dodd|first=Ari|last=Melber|work=[[The Huffington Post]]|date=February 25, 2007|access-date=February 8, 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.thepolitic.org/index.php?option=com_content&task=view&id=77&Itemid=38|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071225193654/http://www.thepolitic.org/index.php?option=com_content&task=view&id=77&Itemid=38|url-status=dead|archive-date=December 25, 2007|title="Ned Who" No More: An interview with Ned Lamont|first=Christopher|last=Gombeski|date=November 10, 2007|access-date=August 15, 2008|work=The Politic}}</ref> After Dodd dropped out of the race, Lamont became a state co-chair for [[Barack Obama]]'s [[Barack Obama 2008 presidential campaign|presidential campaign]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.huffingtonpost.com/ned-lamont/why-im-supporting-barack_b_80986.html|work=[[The Huffington Post]]|first=Ned|last=Lamont|title=Why I'm Supporting Barack Obama|date=March 28, 2008|access-date=February 8, 2013}}</ref> Obama's victory in the [[2008 Connecticut Democratic primary|Connecticut Democratic primary]] was credited to Lamont's ability to turn out the voter base he had built during his Senate campaign.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2008/02/06/us/politics/06region.html?ref=nyregion|work=[[The New York Times]]|title=Obama Takes Connecticut, Helped by Lamont Voters|first=David W.|last=Chen|date=February 6, 2008|access-date=May 20, 2010}}</ref> In March 2008, he was a state delegate to the [[2008 Democratic National Convention]], his support pledged to Obama.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.thegreenpapers.com/P08/CT-D.phtml|work=The Green Papers|title=Connecticut Democratic Delegation 2008|date=February 5, 2008|access-date=July 17, 2013}}</ref>[[File:Ned Lamont and Merle Harris (2557042728) (cropped).jpg|thumb|220px|Lamont speaking with former CCSU president Merle Harris in June 2008]]
==Governor of Connecticut (2019–present)==
===Elections===
====2010====
{{main|2010 Connecticut gubernatorial election}}

On February 16, 2010, Lamont announced his candidacy for the 2010 gubernatorial election.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://ctmirror.org/2010/02/16/lamont-announces-governor-pitching-himself-outsider/|title=Lamont announces for governor, pitching himself as an outsider|first=Mark|last=Pazniokas|work=[[Connecticut Mirror]]|date=February 16, 2010|access-date=August 29, 2010}}</ref> Former [[Stamford, Connecticut|Stamford]] mayor [[Dannel Malloy]] defeated him at the state Democratic convention on May 22, with 1,232 votes (68%) to Lamont's 582 (32%). Since he won more than 15% of the vote, Lamont was eligible to appear on the primary election ballot.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://ctmirror.org/2010/05/22/democrats-malloy-and-wyman-vs-lamont-and-glassman/|title=Democrats: Malloy and Wyman vs. Lamont and Glassman|work=Connecticut Mirror|date=May 22, 2010 |access-date=June 11, 2010}}</ref> On August 10, he lost the primary to Malloy, receiving 43% of the vote.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2010/08/11/nyregion/11conn.html?_r=0|title=Lamont Loses Connecticut Primary for Governor|date=August 10, 2010|access-date=February 8, 2013|work=[[The New York Times]]|first=Raymond|last=Hernandez}}</ref> Malloy defeated [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] candidate [[Thomas C. Foley]] in the general election.<ref>{{cite news| url=https://www.cbsnews.com/news/tom-foley-concedes-ct-governor-race/ | work=[[CBS News]] | first=Brian | last=Montopoli | title=Tom Foley Concedes CT Governor Race | date=November 8, 2010}}</ref>

====2018====
{{main|2018 Connecticut gubernatorial election}}

[[File:2018 Connecticut gubernatorial election results map by municipality.svg|thumb|General election results by municipality. Shades of blue denote wins for Lamont, red for Stefanowski.]]
On January 17, 2018, Lamont announced his candidacy to succeed Malloy, who was not seeking a third term.<ref>{{cite web|last=Colli|first=George|url=http://wtnh.com/2017/11/28/source-ned-lamont-thinking-seriously-about-run-for-governor/|title=Source: Ned Lamont "thinking seriously" about run for governor|work=[[WTNH]]|date=November 28, 2017|access-date=November 28, 2017|archive-date=April 3, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180403231547/http://wtnh.com/2017/11/28/source-ned-lamont-thinking-seriously-about-run-for-governor/|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name=Lamont2018/> He received the party endorsement at the state convention, and chose former [[Connecticut Secretary of State]] [[Susan Bysiewicz]] as his running mate. While missing the 15% threshold, [[Bridgeport]] [[List of mayors of Bridgeport, Connecticut|mayor]] and [[ex-convict]] [[Joe Ganim]] gathered enough signatures to appear on the Democratic primary ballot.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.courant.com/politics/elections/hc-pol-ganim-qualifies-primary-20180619-story.html|title=Joe Ganim And David Stemerman Qualify For Primaries For Governor |date=June 19, 2018|work=Hartford Courant|first=Neil|last=Vigdor|access-date=November 13, 2018}}</ref> Despite the challenge, Lamont won the primary by over 130,000 votes (a 62.4% margin).<ref name=Lamont2018/><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.courant.com/politics/elections/hc-pol-democratic-convention-nomination-governor-20180519-story.html |title=Second Chances: Democrats Endorse Ned Lamont For Governor, Joe Ganim Plans To Primary |last1=Vigdor |first1=Neil |last2=Altimari |first2=Daniela |website=Hartford Courant |last3=Keating |first3=Chris |last4=Gomez-Aceves |first4=Sandra|date=May 19, 2018|access-date=May 19, 2018}}</ref> He then faced Republican [[Bob Stefanowski]] and independent [[Oz Griebel]] in the general election on November 6. Later that night, Griebel conceded the election; Stefanowski conceded early the next morning.<ref name="Courant2018Win" />

====2022====
{{main|2022 Connecticut gubernatorial election}}

Lamont won reelection to a second term as governor, with Bysiewicz as his running mate. He won the Democratic primary unopposed. He defeated Stefanowski in a rematch of their 2018 election with Lamont winning by a wider margin, becoming the first Democrat to win a gubernatorial election by more than five points in the state since [[1986 Connecticut gubernatorial election|1986]].<ref>{{cite news|title=2022 Connecticut primary results|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/elections/election-results/connecticut/2022-primaries/|date=August 24, 2022 |access-date=August 24, 2022}}</ref>

==== 2026 ====
{{Main|2026 Connecticut gubernatorial election}}
Lamont is seeking a third term.<ref>{{Cite web |last= Pazniokas |first= Mark |date= 2025-11-14 |title= Today, the curtain goes up on Ned Lamont's bid for a third term |url= https://ctmirror.org/2025/11/14/gov-lamont-reelection-third-term-announcement/ |access-date= 2026-07-12 |website= CT Mirror |language= en-US}}</ref>

===Administration===
[[File:Gov. Lamont honors Bradley ANG Base firefighters (3).jpg|thumb|Lamont visiting the [[Connecticut Air National Guard]] after the [[2019 Boeing B-17 Flying Fortress crash]]]]
Lamont was sworn in as the 89th governor of Connecticut on January 9, 2019, succeeding [[Dannel Malloy]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.theday.com/article/20181107/NWS12/181109564|title=Stefanowski concedes race to Lamont: 'He won fair and square'|first1=Mark|last1=Pazniokas|first2=Keith M.|last2=Phaneuf|work=The Day|date=November 7, 2018|access-date=November 8, 2018|archive-date=November 7, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181107225723/https://www.theday.com/article/20181107/NWS12/181109564|url-status=dead}}</ref> Some of his top priorities upon taking office included implementing [[Electronic toll collection|electronic tolls]] on state highways; taxing [[streaming media|online streaming services]]; restoring the [[property tax]] [[tax credit|credit]]; [[Cannabis in Connecticut|legalizing marijuana]] for recreational use; increasing the [[Minimum wage in the United States|minimum wage]] to $15 per hour; instituting paid [[Parental leave|family]] and [[Sick leave|medical leave]]; renegotiating contracts with [[Public-sector trade union|public-sector unions]]; and legalizing [[sports betting]].<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.courant.com/politics/hc-pol-lamont-campaign-promises-20181116-story.html|title=Gov.-elect Ned Lamont made a lot of campaign promises. Which ones might happen?|first=Christopher|last=Keating|work=Hartford Courant|date=November 18, 2018|access-date=November 21, 2018}}</ref> Lamont also prioritized investments in rail infrastructure, proposing shorter travel times between cities by upgrading rail lines, as well as extending the [[Danbury Branch]] to [[New Milford station|New Milford]] and re-electrifying the line.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.courant.com/politics/hc-pol-ned-lamont-state-of-the-state-highlights-20190109-6nut5qb3cbd3xclsfizzepho5a-story.html|title=7 key lines from Ned Lamont's State of the State speech|work=Hartford Courant|first=Russell|last=Blair|date=January 9, 2019|access-date=January 10, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.newstimes.com/news/article/Gubernatorial-candidate-Ned-Lamont-talks-transit-542517.php|title=Gubernatorial candidate Ned Lamont talks transit at Danbury train station|first=Dirk|last=Perrefort|work=Danbury News-Times|date=June 30, 2010|access-date=January 10, 2019}}</ref>

====First term (2019−2023)====
In February 2019, Lamont appointed former [[PepsiCo]] CEO [[Indra Nooyi]] co-director of the Connecticut Economic Resource Center (CERC), a [[public-private partnership]] with the Department of Economic and Community Development tasked with revamping the state's economic development strategy. A year later, CERC rebranded itself as AdvanceCT. In April 2019, Lamont signed his first [[executive order]], which directed state office buildings and vehicle fleets to become more energy-efficient through an expanded "Lead By Example Sustainability Initiative". The initiative aims to reduce both the carbon footprint and cost of state government operations. In June 2019, Lamont signed a bill that banned [[gay panic defense]] in Connecticut, and also signed three gun-control bills, including Ethan's Law, which requires safely storing firearms in households where children are present, bans [[privately made firearm]]s, and bans storing unlocked guns in unattended vehicles.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Carlesso |first=Maya Moore, Jenna |date=2019-06-07 |title=Lamont signs gun safety bills, reaffirms commitment to ending violence |url=https://ctmirror.org/2019/06/07/lamont-signs-gun-safety-bills-reaffirms-commitment-to-ending-violence/ |access-date=2026-06-22 |website=CT Mirror |language=en-US}}</ref>

On June 26, Lamont signed his first biennium state budget, amounting to $43 billion. Because of the state's heavy reliance on business tax revenue, specifically from the finance and insurance sectors, Connecticut had struggled with large fluctuations and shortfalls in revenue.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.theatlantic.com/business/archive/2017/07/connecticut-tax-inequality-cities/532623/|title = What on Earth is Wrong with Connecticut?|website = [[The Atlantic]]|date = July 5, 2017}}</ref> To balance the budget (as legally required) without increasing [[income tax]]es, two state pension funds were restructured to reduce expenses for two years. $170 million in transportation spending was cut, numerous taxes and fees increased, and several previously approved tax cuts canceled. This included an additional $50 million of taxes on LLCs and other small and mid-sized businesses, a 10% tax increase on alcoholic beverages and a 1% surcharge on restaurant food and other prepared meals. The budget also assumed the administration would save almost $460 million over two years by collaborating with state employee unions. Personal income tax rates did not increase in either of the biennium budgets Lamont signed.

Despite these cuts and tax increases, the budget increased funding for education and workforce development, maintained municipal aid funding so mayors and first selectmen had stability in local budgets, and included $2.2 billion in the state's [[rainy day fund]],<ref name="ctmirror.org">{{Cite web|date= June 26, 2019| title= Lamont signs new budget, says it will bring stability to CT| url= https://ctmirror.org/2019/06/26/lamont-signs-new-budget-says-it-will-bring-stability-to-ct/ |access-date= November 15, 2021|website= The CT Mirror |language= en-US}}</ref> the largest in state history at the time.<ref>{{Cite web|date= June 26, 2019|title=Lamont signs 2-year, $43 billion state budget into law| url= https://www.fox61.com/article/news/local/outreach/awareness-months/lamont-signs-2-year-43-billion-state-budget-into-law/520-cfa8bd47-44b4-466d-ace7-fbb8156091db|access-date= November 15, 2021|website= fox61.com|language= en-US}}</ref> Lamont said, "It's a budget that I think gets us back on track, holds the line on spending, holds the line on taxes. It gives people a great deal of confidence they know what's going on." On March 8, 2020, Connecticut reported its first confirmed [[COVID-19]] case. As cases rose, Lamont declared a public health emergency. He limited visits to nursing homes and ordered all schools to close on March 16. Lamont issued an executive order closing all non-essential businesses on March 20 and issued a stay-at-home order on March 23. As cases continued to rise, he limited gathering sizes to five people and mandated face coverings in public. On May 20, Lamont lifted the stay-at-home order as cases began to subside. In the summer, non-essential businesses such as barber shops, gyms, and malls began to open. In the fall, cases and deaths began to rise again in the state. The state kept the mask mandate in place.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Staff|first=Hearst Connecticut Media|title=From day 1 to now: Key moments in Connecticut's COVID battle|url=https://www.ctpost.com/projects/2020/covid-19-in-ct-timeline/|access-date=November 18, 2021|website=Connecticut Post|language=en-US}}</ref>

In July 2020, Lamont signed into law a sweeping police reform bill that requires all police officers to be equipped with [[Police body camera|body cameras]], prohibits maneuvers such as [[chokeholds]], creates a statewide watchdog for [[police misconduct]], limits police departments' ability to withhold disciplinary records, and makes individual officers liable in civil lawsuits. On December 15, 2020, the first person in the state received the COVID-19 vaccine. In coordination with the federal government and private hospitals, Connecticut became the first state to vaccinate 50% of its adult population. On May 19, 2021, Lamont ended most of the state's restrictions. Bars, theaters, and ballparks were all able to open, but people were encouraged to wear masks. On May 29, Lamont signed a bill raising the state minimum hourly wage to $11 that year in October and $15 by 2023. Also in May 2021, he signed into law a bill to legalize online sports betting and gaming. His office negotiated the deal's terms with the General Assembly, the Mohegan Tribe, and the Mashantucket Pequot Tribe.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Singer|first=Stephen|title=Gov. Ned Lamont and tribal owners of the Mohegan Sun and Foxwoods strike a deal bringing sports betting and online gambling to Connecticut|url=https://www.courant.com/politics/hc-pol-online-gambling-tribes-agreement-20210318-kyxv7aogsnabxlqre746wkkzg4-story.html|access-date=November 15, 2021|website=courant.com|date=March 18, 2021 }}</ref>

In June 2021, Lamont signed his second biennium state budget, amounting to $46 billion. The balanced budget, passed with bipartisan support,<ref name="ReferenceA">{{Cite web|date=June 9, 2021|title=CT House passes $46B budget that avoids tax hikes|url=https://ctmirror.org/2021/06/08/house-expected-to-pass-46b-budget-that-avoids-tax-hikes/|access-date=November 15, 2021|website=The CT Mirror|language=en-US}}</ref> expanded HUSKY A health insurance for 40,000 people, increased the state's rainy day fund to $4.5 billion, and increased the state's earned income tax credit for low-income earners to 30% of the federal credit, up from 23%,<ref>{{Cite web|last=Keating|first=Christopher|title=No tax increases in Connecticut state budget deal as Gov. Lamont and legislators reach general agreement on $46 billion budget.|url=https://www.courant.com/politics/hc-pol-connecticut-budget-deal-no-taxes-20210604-v3vhmtksyrfgxezf7mmvrvtkge-story.html|access-date=November 15, 2021|website=courant.com|date=June 4, 2021 }}</ref> and also legalized [[Cannabis in Connecticut|recreational marijuana usage]] for adults and created a legal framework for retailers. Connecticut is the 19th state to end [[Cannabis in the United States|cannabis]] prohibition. On June 25, Lamont signed into law an expansive family and medical leave program. Starting in 2022, Connecticut workers will be able to take up to 12 weeks of paid leave. Workers will be eligible for paid time off to care for a newborn, a seriously ill relative, or their own medical issue. The plan is paid for by a 0.5% payroll tax on all employees.<ref>{{Cite web|last=Altimari|first=Daniela|title=Gov. Ned Lamont just signed a sweeping family and medical leave law. How will it work?|url=https://www.courant.com/politics/hc-pol-cla-family-leave-connecticut-20190625-tyk3pusbwnhqjfvhrscgkbbcim-story.html|access-date=November 15, 2021|website=courant.com|date=June 25, 2019 }}</ref>

In July 2021, Lamont signed a highway user fee on large commercial transports. The measure charges 2.5 cents per mile for vehicles weighing 26,000 to 28,000 pounds and 17.5 cents per mile for trucks weighing more than 80,000 pounds.<ref>{{Cite web|date=June 9, 2021|title=Legislature passes highway usage tax on large commercial trucks|url=https://ctmirror.org/2021/06/08/house-passes-highway-usage-tax-on-large-commercial-trucks/|access-date=November 15, 2021|website=The CT Mirror|language=en-US}}</ref> The revenue is to be used to help repair state roads and highways.<ref>{{Cite web|last=McQuaid|first=Hugh|date=July 13, 2021|title=Highway User Fee Signed|url=http://ctnewsjunkie.com/2021/07/13/highway-user-fee-signed/|access-date=November 15, 2021|website=CTNewsJunkie|language=en-US}}</ref> Connecticut ranked 24th in CNBC's annual ''Top States for Business'' study, up from 35th in the previous study, conducted in 2019. The state's ranking was driven by improvements in business friendliness, access to capital, infrastructure, and economy categories.<ref>{{Cite web|last=staff|first=CNBC com|date=July 13, 2021|title=24. Connecticut|url=https://www.cnbc.com/2021/07/13/top-states-for-business-connecticut.html|access-date=November 15, 2021|website=CNBC|language=en}}</ref> In October 2021, sports betting and online gaming officially launched. Bets can be made online at sites such as [[DraftKings]] and [[FanDuel Sports Network|FanDuel]] and at a select few locations, including Mohegan Sun and Foxwoods Casino.<ref>{{Cite web|author=WFSB Staff|title=Your guide to sports betting in CT|url=https://www.wfsb.com/news/your-guide-to-sports-betting-in-ct/article_62be608a-213a-11ec-a3d7-eb399f0dfb28.html|access-date=November 15, 2021|website=WFSB|language=en|archive-date=November 15, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211115201928/https://www.wfsb.com/news/your-guide-to-sports-betting-in-ct/article_62be608a-213a-11ec-a3d7-eb399f0dfb28.html|url-status=dead}}</ref>

In November 2021, at least 80% of people in the state, including children who had just been approved to receive the vaccine by the FDA, were partially vaccinated,<ref>{{Cite web|title=CDC reports Connecticut ranks first in child vaccinations|url=https://www.wfsb.com/news/cdc-reports-connecticut-ranks-first-in-child-vaccinations/article_e4e8068a-3060-11ec-9eeb-379710d49231.html|access-date=November 6, 2021|website=WFSB|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|title=The States With the Best COVID-19 Vaccination Rates|url=https://www.usnews.com/news/best-states/articles/these-states-have-the-best-covid-19-vaccination-rates}}</ref> and administration of Connecticut's [[CARES Act]] and [[American Rescue Plan Act of 2021|American Rescue Plan Act]] funds for economic recovery and stimulus.<ref>{{Cite web|title=The federal stimulus package will send $10 billion to Connecticut. Here's how much funding your town will get|url=https://www.courant.com/news/connecticut/hc-news-stimulus-money-is-coming-to-connecticut-20210311-uwn2to36cvbgtnes4wqvbwafdu-story.html|access-date=November 6, 2021|website=courant.com|date=March 11, 2021 }}</ref> According to the 2021 analysis of Connecticut's business environment the Connecticut Business & Industry Association conducts annually, business confidence was up 7% from the previous year while perception that the state's business climate was on the decline dropped by 15% from the previous year.<ref>{{Cite web|title=2021 Survey of Connecticut Businesses|url=https://www.cbia.com/resources/economy/reports-surveys/2021-survey-of-connecticut-businesses/|access-date=November 15, 2021|website=CBIA|date=September 30, 2021 |language=en-US}}</ref> 

On May 5, 2022, Lamont signed into law [[House Bill 5414]], which was intended to protect [[abortion in Connecticut]]. It also prevents Connecticut judges from suing any person or corporation for receiving or performing an abortion in the state.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Connecticut governor signs law protecting abortion seekers and providers from out-of-state lawsuits |url=https://www.cnn.com/2022/05/05/politics/connecticut-abortion-protection-law-out-of-state-lawsuits/index.html |website=CNN|date=May 5, 2022 |language=en-US}}</ref>

==== Second term (2023−present) ====
[[File:Connecticut Governor Ned Lamont sworn in for his second term in Hartford (cropped).jpg|thumb|Lamont is sworn into his second term at the [[State Arsenal and Armory|State Arsenal and Armony]].]]
Lamont defeated Stefanowski again to win a second term in 2022. His second swearing-in took place on January 4, 2023, at the [[State Arsenal and Armory]], along with Lieutenant Governor [[Susan Bysiewicz]] and constitutional officers.<ref name="second term">{{Cite web |date=January 4, 2023 |title=Connecticut Gov. Ned Lamont sworn into office, seeks tax cut |url=https://apnews.com/article/politics-connecticut-covid-pandemics-7116f69e758d6e992801c44226e0d7f1 |access-date=January 14, 2023 |website=AP NEWS |language=en}}</ref>

On July 19, 2023, Lamont signed into law a series of bills aimed at protecting reproductive rights and increasing public access to birth control in Connecticut. These included Public Act 23-128, which protects medical providers from receiving "adverse actions" another state may try to enforce. It also protects licensed medical providers from having their licenses revoked, suspended, or denied renewal if they provide reproductive services to patients, including those who come from a state where abortion is illegal.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 19, 2024 |title=Lamont signs new laws protecting reproductive rights |url=https://www.fox61.com/article/news/politics/connecticut-governor-lamont-signs-new-laws-protection-reproductive-rights/520-f752de07-f390-40e3-b78f-7410d3d55496 |access-date=July 25, 2024 |website=AP NEWS |language=en}}</ref>

In June 2025, Lamont vetoed a bill that would have allowed striking workers to receive unemployment assistance. That same month, he vetoed an Omnibus [[YIMBY]] bill that would have expanded [[affordable housing]], streamlined permitting for [[missing middle housing]], and legalized [[transit oriented development]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Savino • • |first=Mike |date=June 23, 2025 |title=Lamont vetoes bills on housing, paying striking workers lost wages |url=https://www.nbcconnecticut.com/news/local/lamont-vetoes-bills-housing-paying-striking-workers-lost-wages/3593440/ |access-date=June 24, 2025 |website=NBC Connecticut |language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |title= |url=https://www.wtnh.com/news/connecticut/gov-ned-lamont-says-he-will-veto-hotly-debated-housing-striking-workers-bills/}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Pazniokas |first=Mark |date=June 23, 2025 |title=Lamont vetoes H.B. 5002 housing bill, sets local 'buy-in' as priority |url=http://ctmirror.org/2025/06/23/lamont-vetoes-h-b-5002-housing-bill-sets-local-buy-in-as-priority/ |access-date=June 24, 2025 |website=CT Mirror |language=en-US}}</ref> In November 2025, it was revealed that Lamont had asked one of his aides to draft a statement announcing his support for the pro-housing housing legislation just 13 days before he vetoed it.<ref name=":0" /> It is unclear why Lamont's views on the legislation changed.<ref name=":0" /> 

Lamont called the [[Assassination of Charlie Kirk|assassination]] of [[Charlie Kirk]] "absolutely awful" and ordered US and state flags in Connecticut lowered to half-staff for him.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Ciaglo |first=Karla |date=September 11, 2025 |title=Connecticut Leaders React To Charlie Kirk Assassination With Condemnation, Gun Reform Calls, and Rare Bipartisan Unity |url=https://ctnewsjunkie.com/2025/09/11/connecticut-leaders-react-to-charlie-kirk-assassination-with-condemnation-gun-reform-calls-and-rare-bipartisan-unity/ |access-date=September 13, 2025 |website=CT News Junkie |language=en-US}}</ref>

===Approval ratings===
[[File:Connecticut governors Ned Lamont and Jodi Rell.jpg|thumb|Former governor [[Jodi Rell]] with Ned Lamont in 2023.]]
In his first year as governor, Lamont garnered consistently low approval ratings. [[Morning Consult]] had listed him among the ten least popular governors every quarter since his election. In a survey conducted in the last quarter of 2019, he was ranked the fourth most unpopular governor in the country, with a 51% disapproval rating and a 32% approval rating.<ref name="MorningConsult">{{cite web|url=https://morningconsult.com/governor-rankings/|title=Morning Consult's Governor Approval Ratings|work=[[Morning Consult]]|date=April 2019|access-date=May 17, 2019}}</ref>

Since the pandemic began in 2020, Lamont's approval ratings have been higher. In a May 2020 [[Quinnipiac poll]], he received a 65% approval rating and a 26% disapproval rating, with a 78% approval rating for his handling of the pandemic.<ref name=QuinnipiacPoll>{{cite web|url=https://ctmirror.org/2020/05/06/q-poll-lamont-popularity-high-ct-respondents-oppose-quick-reopening-of-the-state/|title=Q poll: Lamont popularity high, CT respondents oppose quick reopening of the state|work=[[Quinnipiac University Polling Institute]]|date=May 6, 2020|access-date=June 17, 2020}}</ref> By October 2020, a [[Sacred Heart University]] survey found his overall approval rating to be 53%, while 71% approved of his handling of the pandemic.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Jones |first1=Harriet |title=SHU: Lamont's Approval Rating Rises |url=https://www.wnpr.org/post/shu-lamonts-approval-rating-rises |website=Connecticut Public Radio|date=October 30, 2020|access-date=December 22, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|title=Lamont Approval Is Up Since April|url=https://www.wshu.org/post/lamont-approval-april#stream/0|first=Ebong|last=Udoma|date=November 3, 2020|access-date=March 1, 2021|work=WSHU Public Radio}}</ref> In a March 2021 Sacred Heart University poll, 70.7% of people approved of Lamont's handling of the pandemic and his approval stood at nearly 56%, almost double his 28.1% approval from the same pollster in December 2019.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Blair |first1=Russell |title=Poll: More than 70% approve of Gov. Ned Lamont's handling of COVID-19; overall approval rating reaches new high |url=https://www.courant.com/politics/capitol-watch/hc-pol-lamont-approval-rating-20210304-5y2ag2nwqvfjjlvkflbsh2aakq-story.html |access-date=March 4, 2021 |publisher=Hartford Courant |date=March 4, 2021}}</ref> In 2022, Lamont had the highest approval rating of any Democratic governor.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Putterman |first=Alex |date=July 21, 2022 |title=Gov. Ned Lamont has highest approval rating of any Democratic governor, new poll finds |url=https://www.ctinsider.com/news/article/Gov-Ned-Lamont-has-highest-approval-rating-of-17318268.php |access-date=January 15, 2023 |website=CT Insider |language=en-US}}</ref>

==Personal life==
On September 10, 1983,<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/1983/07/17/style/ann-huntress-to-wed-em-lamont-jr.html|title=Ann Huntress to Wed E.M. Lamont Jr.|date=July 17, 1983|work=The New York Times|access-date=October 22, 2018}}</ref> Lamont married [[Ann Lamont|Ann Huntress]], a [[venture capital]]ist and managing partner at [[Oak Investment Partners]].<ref name="moffly"/><ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2006/10/16/nyregion/16wife.html?pagewanted=all&_r=0|title=Not-So-Hidden Asset, His Wife, Is Force in Lamont's Senate Bid|date=October 16, 2006|access-date=February 8, 2013|first=Alison Leigh|last=Cowan|work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref> They have three children.<ref name="moffly"/> He and his family live in [[Greenwich, Connecticut|Greenwich]] and have a [[vacation home]] in [[North Haven, Maine]].<ref name="Kershaw"/> The [[Lamont Gallery]] on the campus of [[Phillips Exeter Academy]] and the [[Lamont Library]] at [[Harvard University]] are both named in honor of his family.<ref name="Kershaw" />

